.TH CCOL_CHANNEL_CREATE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_channel_create \- create a two-way communication ccol_channel
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_channel_create(max_size, err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro creates a two-way ccol_channel. Inside, the channel holds two
circular queues that carry messages between an owner thread and the
worker threads, and both directions have the same capacity. The macro
uses the standard malloc and free for memory management.

This macro is the short form of ccol_channel_create_with_mprocs() with NULL
memory management procedures.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
max_size   Maximum number of messages that each direction can hold.
           Because one circular queue holds each direction, the same
           SIZE_MAX / sizeof(c_message_t) bound that
           ccol_circular_queue_create(3) documents applies here.
err_str    Optional pointer for an error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
On success, the macro gives a pointer to the new ccol_channel.
On failure, it gives NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
All operations are thread-safe.
You must destroy the channel with ccol_channel_destroy() after you finish
with it.
The channel picks the direction itself from the ID of the thread that
calls it.
The owner thread is the thread that called ccol_channel_create(), and it
must stay alive for all the time that the ccol_channel is in use. To
route a message, the channel compares the ID of the calling thread with
the ID that it took at creation time. After the first owner exits, the
OS can give the same ID to a new, unrelated thread; the channel then
routes that thread as the owner too, and it reports nothing.
There are two supported ways to combine fork(2) with a ccol_channel:
call fork(2) before you create the ccol_channel, or create the
ccol_channel, then call fork(2) and immediately call exec(3). One
pattern is not supported: a program that continues to run with no
exec(3) in between while a ccol_channel from before the fork is live.
On top of that rule, the library adds a defense in depth: the child
never inherits a lock in the locked state from another thread that
held it at the instant of the fork.  Because neither queue below the
channel owns a thread of its own, a ccol_channel that a child inherits
in that way stays fully usable.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_channel_create_with_mprocs (3),
.BR ccol_channel_destroy (3)
